s rights protections  were at first actively pursued and  instantiated individual religious groups  issued bold confessional statements and  manifestos on human rights shortly after  World War two several denominations and  budding ecumenical bodies joined in the  cultivation of human rights at the  international level the free church  tradition in particular played a  critical role in the civil rights  movement in America as did Christian  Democratic Catholic movements in Europe  and in Latin America and in parts of  francophone Africa but after expressing  some initial interest leaders of the  rights revolution consigned religious  groups and their unique religious rights  claims to a lower and lower priority  freedom of speech and press parity of  race and gender provision of work and  welfare captured most of the energy and  emoluments of the rights revolution  after the 1960s and early 1970s academic  inquiries and activist interventions  into religious rights and their abuses  became increasingly intermittent and  isolated new constitutional laws emerged  in the United States and in Western  Europe that began to demand that laws  and legal instruments be grounded in  secular purposes and directed the  secular ends in order to pass  constitutional muster the rights  revolution both in international law  and in a variety of domestic laws seemed  to be passing and religion by and this  deprecation of the special roles and the  special rights of religions in the last  third of the 20th century or so has  introduced a number of distortions and  ooh the current theory of human rights  that tends to dominate discussions today  first without religion many rights are  cut from their roots the right to  religion the great German jurist York  Jelena crowed a century ago is the  mother of many other rights for the  religious individual the right to  believe and to exercise that belief  leads ineluctably to the rights to  assemble to speak through travel to  worship to proselytize to educate to  parents and other actions taken on the  direct foundation of those beliefs for  the religious group the right to exist  invariably involves rights to corporate  organization group property collective  worship organized charity parochial  education freedom of press autonomy of  governance transnational contracts and  much more to ignore religious rights is  often to overlook the conceptual source  of many other individual and  associational rights and it's also to  ignore the historical source of many of  our most cherished constitutional  freedoms  it's an instructive anecdote for example  that by 1650 every one of the rights  that would eventually appear in the  American Bill of Rights of 1789 had  already been fully theoretically  formulated and defended by Christian  writers who were elaborating what they  consider to be the essential correlative  rights of the Cardinal right to freedom  of conscience and freedom of exercise  and if one looks in the post colonial  post autocratic regimes oftentimes the  formulation of the religious rights of  the individual and the religious rights  of the group provide the constitutional  traction for the elaboration of a  variety of correlative rights that are  attached thereto and eventually  constitutionally and Stan's  second without religion the regime of  human rights is in danger of becoming  infinitely expandable the classic faiths  of the world community adopt and  advocate human rights in parts in order  to protect religious duties a religious  individual or a religious Association  has rights to exist  axé not in the abstract but in order to  discharge discrete duties defined by  their faith commitments and faith  communities rights and duties properly  belong together  in most religious traditions to speak of  one without the other is ultimately  destructive rights without duties to  guide them quickly become claims of  self-indulgence duties without rights to  exercise them quickly become sources of  deep guilt religious traditions amongst  others offer some of the best examples  of the organic linkage between rights  and duties and thereby put a natural  check and balance on the limitless  expansion of human rights into simple  into a simple gaggle of ungrounded  whimsical wishes and wants that have no  real ontological foundation and no real  chance of becoming real in practice  third without religion the state is  given an exaggerated role to play as the  guarantor of human rights the simple  state versus individual dialectic of  many modern human rights theories leaves  it to the state to protect provide and  vindicate rights of all sorts in reality  the state is not and cannot be so Amna  competent as the failed socialist  experiments of the 20th century have  tragically shown us numerous mediating  structures or spheres of justice stand  between the state and the individual  religious communities prominently  amongst them religious institutions or  Communities amongst many others play a  vital role in the cultivation and in the  realization of many types of rights they  can create the conditions and sometimes  the prototypes for some of the first  generations civil and political rights  they can provide a critical and in some  communities the principal means to meet  second-generation rights of education  health care childcare labor employment  artistic opportunities and much more and  religious communities offer some of the  deepest insights into the norms of  creation stewardship and servant  that lie at the heart of what we now  call third generation rights of peace  orderly development environmental  protection
 

